THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001's canonical witness stores the *complete* set of accepted defenders defeating each attacker. This proposal may violate the determinism guarantee when the argument framework permits multiple alternative defender sets: because no minimality is required, the 'complete set' is uniquely the union of all accepted defenders, which is deterministic. However, when the framework contains symmetric attacks, an accepted defender x of attacker y may itself be attacked by y, and x is in the grounded extension only if y is refuted — yet y attacks x, so x's acceptance depends on y's status, creating a circularity. The bitset over the finite set encodes this circularity as if it were a static witness, but the actual defense relation is not acyclic. The fixpoint is still deterministic, but the stored witness may certify a node whose acceptance depends on a defender that is only accepted because the node itself is accepted, which violates the validity of committed roots because the defender's acceptance is not independent of the defended node.

TARGET:
Replace the per-node greedy DefenseWitness materialization with a canonical witness that stores, for each accepted node x, the complete set of accepted defenders that defeat each attacker of x, encoded as a bitset over the finite argument set. This set is uniquely determined by the grounded fixpoint and does not depend on enumeration order, so it avoids SRC_008's refutation. It is still a valid defense because every attacker is defeated by at least one member of the set; no minimality is claimed. Polynomial cost holds because each node's attacker-defender relation is already computed during fixpoint and bitset operations are linear in graph size. Criticism: full sets can be large, and the witness may include redundant defenders, but this is acceptable if the witness is not required to be minimal.